it was quite a chunky manifesto 86 pages  in total almost 16 measures not many are  what you'd call brand-new but certainly  some of them are striking so let's have  a look through a few of them starting  with what comes on page 1 of the  document the label will cut the deficit  every single year of the new Parliament  and in an effort to underscore their  fiscal responsibility they say  everything in the manifesto is costed  which implies cut across the board  except in education health and foreign  aid they promised that they won't raise  taxes like v80 but they will reintroduce  the 50p tax rate for those earning over  150,000 pounds another new promise that  we heard from Miliband today is that he  said he'll extend free childcare for  working parents of three and  four-year-olds from 15 to 25 hours and  he'll double paternity leave 24 weeks  that's going to be paid for in part by  an increase in the bank levy and here's  another promise an extra 2.5 billion  pounds to recruit more staff in the NHS  which labor save will be paid for by a  mansion tax on property is worth over 2  million pounds but they have  subsequently said that they won't match  the conservative pledge of 8 billion  pounds of NHS spending by 2020 but most  of those policies don't carry explicit  costings there's a fast of rising  minimum wage to 8 pounds an hour by 2019  rail fares they're going to be frozen  for a year and there's going to be a new  primary child care service to help with  working families and they're going to  raise some of the money from other tax  changes like cutting the winter fuel  allowance for the top 5% of pensioners  and as we heard last week they're going  to abolish non Dom status though it's  unclear whether that's how you're going  to raise money or cost it Mr Miliband  says the rest of the money is going to  have to come from the non-protected  departments in other words the Home  Office culture and defense and other  parts of government as well though  there's very few numbers on the scale of  those cuts in the document itself on the  labor side the uncertainty is not so  much about where the cuts would come but  actually whether there would be and what  the scale  the cuts or tax rises will be over the  first half of the Parliament because the  fiscal rules they've set themselves are  consistent either with actually nothing  in the way of spending cuts or tax rises  or with over the first two or three  years some really significant tax  increases or spending cuts and we don't  actually know still which path the  Labour Party want to tread finally there  are still some who suspect ed Miliband  has a big policy held back and he might  have some surprises left for the final  weeks of this election campaign
 

